{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: When history shows a sharp price recovery (e.g., rising from ~68 to >80), the model's flat or linearly increasing forecast fails to capture the magnitude of the spike. Apply a positive bias adjustment of 10-15 EUR for the first 10 days of a recovery phase.",
    "Apply aggressive mean reversion after peaks: The model consistently overshoots the peak and fails to anticipate the subsequent sharp decline (e.g., dropping from ~80 to ~63). Implement a negative correction of 5-8 EUR starting 10-15 days after the forecasted peak to align with the observed rapid mean reversion.",
    "Ignore low sentiment signals during volatility: Sentiment history remains near zero (0.00) even during significant price swings (e.g., 68 to 80 to 63). Do not rely on sentiment as a leading indicator for trend changes in this regime; instead, prioritize momentum-based adjustments based on the last 5 days of price action.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon bias: The model exhibits a persistent 'flat-line' bias, assuming stability where high volatility exists. Reduce the forecast slope by 50% and introduce a rolling mean-reversion component to prevent the forecast from drifting away from the true cyclical range.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend reversal: The model lags the true trend reversal by approximately 3-5 days. When the 5-day moving average of history crosses the current price, immediately adjust the forecast downward (if price is falling) or upward (if price is rising) by the magnitude of the recent daily change."
  ]
}
